/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Shintaro Uda built and studied the array in Japan in the 1920s and Hidetsugu Yagi published it in English, so both names stay.
   - Each parasitic rod carries a current induced by the driven element’s field and re-radiates.
   - The phases come from lengths and spacings, so they are right only near one frequency.
   - A log-periodic antenna repeats its geometry at sizes in a fixed ratio, so its behaviour at frequency f repeats at f/τ, f/τ², and so on; if the steps are small, the properties hardly change in between.
   - τ = Ln+1/Ln = dn+1/dn
   - τ is the scale factor (less than 1, typically 0.8 to 0.95); Ln is the length of dipole n counted from the longest; dn is the gap between dipoles n and n + 1.
   - σ is the spacing factor, typically 0.06 to 0.2; the half apex angle is α = tan⁻¹[(1 − τ)/(4σ)].
   - N = 1 + ln(B)/ln(1/τ), B = fmax/fmin
   - N is the number of dipoles, rounded up; B is the bandwidth ratio.
   - All dipoles are fed from a two-wire line along the boom, with the connections crossed between neighbours, and the cable is connected at the short front end.
*/
