/* book Worked example 2: Q: An LPDA must cover 200 to 600 MHz with τ = 0.9, σ = 0.15 and a longest dipole of 750 mm. Find the number of dipoles, the shortest dipole, the half apex angle, the first spacing and the boom length. Which dipoles are active at 300 MHz?
   1. B = 600/200 = 3. ln 3 = 1.0986 and ln(1/0.9) = 0.10536, so N = 1 + 10.43 = 11.43, rounded up to 12.
   2. Check L1: λ/2 at 200 MHz is 1500/2 = 750 mm, as given.
   3. Shortest dipole: L12 = 750 × 0.9¹¹ = 750 × 0.3138 = 235.4 mm; it is half a wavelength at 150/0.2354 = 637 MHz, above 600 MHz, so the top of the band is covered.
   4. Half apex angle: tan α = (1 − 0.9)/(4 × 0.15) = 0.1667, so α = 9.46°.
   5. First spacing: d1 = 2σL1 = 2 × 0.15 × 750 = 225 mm; then 202.5, 182.3, … down to d11 = 78.5 mm.
   6. Boom: (L1 − L12)/(2 tan α) = (750 − 235.4)/(2 × 0.1667) = 1544 mm, the same as the sum of the 11 spacings.
   7. At 300 MHz, λ/2 = 500 mm, which lies between dipole 4 (546.8 mm) and dipole 5 (492.1 mm); the active region is dipoles 3 to 6.
   Answer: 12 dipoles from 750 mm down to 235 mm, half apex angle 9.5°, first spacing 225 mm, boom about 1.54 m; at 300 MHz dipoles 4 and 5 do most of the radiating. */
