/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Across the opening the field has an amplitude and a phase at every point; this aperture field is the source of the far field.
   - Ae is the effective aperture in m²; A is the physical area of the opening in m²; ηap is the aperture efficiency (0 to 1), which counts taper and phase error.
   - D is the directivity as a ratio; λ is the free-space wavelength in m.
   - A rectangular waveguide fed in the TE10 mode already has a cosine taper across its broad wall, so even a perfect horn opening has ηap ≈ 0.81 from taper alone; the phase error of a practical flare brings it down to about 0.5.
   - Flaring only the walls in the plane of the electric field (the narrow walls of the TE10 guide) gives an E-plane sectoral horn; flaring only the broad walls, in the plane of the magnetic field, gives an H-plane sectoral horn; flaring both gi
   - The wave inside the flare spreads from the apex as a cylindrical or spherical front, so it reaches the edge of the opening later than the centre.
   - ℓE = B²/(2λ), ℓH = A²/(3λ)
   - A is the H-plane width and B the E-plane height of the opening; ℓE and ℓH are the apex-to-aperture lengths for optimum flares.
   - HPBWE ≈ 56λ/B, HPBWH ≈ 67λ/A
   - Half-power beamwidths in degrees of an optimum horn.
*/
