/* book Worked example: Q: A pyramidal horn at 10 GHz has an opening A = 10 cm (H-plane) by B = 8 cm (E-plane) and aperture efficiency 0.5. Find the gain in dBi, the two half-power beamwidths, and the apex lengths that make both flares optimum.
   1. λ = 3 × 10⁸/10¹⁰ = 0.03 m = 3 cm. A = 0.10 × 0.08 = 0.0080 m²; Ae = 0.5 × 0.0080 = 0.0040 m².
   2. G = 4πAe/λ² = 4π × 0.0040/(0.03)² = 0.05027/0.0009 = 55.9, and 10 log 55.9 = 17.5 dBi.
   3. HPBWE ≈ 56λ/B = 56 × 3/8 = 21.0°; HPBWH ≈ 67λ/A = 67 × 3/10 = 20.1°.
   4. Optimum E-plane length: ℓE = B²/(2λ) = 64/6 = 10.7 cm. Check δ = B²/(8ℓE) = 64/85.3 = 0.75 cm = 0.25λ.
   5. Optimum H-plane length: ℓH = A²/(3λ) = 100/9 = 11.1 cm. Check δ = 100/(8 × 11.1) = 1.125 cm = 0.375λ.
   6. Unit check: the area went into the formula in m² and λ² in m², so the ratio has no unit.
   Answer: G = 55.9, that is 17.5 dBi; beamwidths about 21.0° (E-plane) and 20.1° (H-plane); apex lengths about 10.7 cm (E) and 11.1 cm (H) for optimum flares. The near-square beam comes from the larger H-plane width offsetting its taper. */
