/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A parabola is the set of points equally far from a fixed point, the focus, and a fixed line, the directrix.
   - Every ray from the focus reflects parallel to the axis, and every such ray travels the same total distance from the focus to any plane across the mouth.
   - ψ0 is the half angle the rim subtends at the focus: the feed must light ±ψ0.
   - Prime focus: a horn at the focus looks back at the dish.
   - The feed’s own pattern decides how the dish is lit.
   - G is the gain as a ratio; η is the aperture efficiency; D is the diameter and λ the wavelength in the same unit.
   - HPBW is the half-power beamwidth and BWFN the beamwidth between first nulls, in degrees. 70λ/D suits the usual tapered feed; a uniformly lit circular aperture gives 58λ/D for the half-power width.
   - Two scaling facts follow.
*/
